<p>In humans, each eye has a small blind spot corresponding to the point where the optic nerve leaves the retina. Normally this isn&#8217;t noticeable because the brain can fill in the gap with information from the other eye. But by closing one eye and positioning the target at the appropriate angle, it&#8217;s possible to invoke the gap deliberately.</p>
<p>&#8220;When the famous physiological psychologist Karl S. Lashley was forced to endure an irritating dinner guest, he amused himself by bringing his blind spot over the person&#8217;s head, neatly decapitating the person.&#8221;</p>
<p>&#8212; Jearl Walker, <em>The Flying Circus of Physics</em>, 2006</p>
